## Why This Is Asked
Explores dynamic extensibility and runtime hot-swapping of behavior without redeployments, crucial for scalable data platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Plugin registry mapping (type, version) to handlers
- Versioned command schemas and graceful deprecation
- Isolation via worker pools or process boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal interface sketch (TypeScript-like)
interface Command {
  execute(ctx: any, payload: any): Promise<void>;
}
interface PluginFactory {
  create(payload: any): Command;
}
class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, Map<number, PluginFactory>>();

  register(type: string, version: number, factory: PluginFactory) {
    if (!this.map.has(type)) {
      this.map.set(type, new Map());
    }
    this.map.get(type)!.set(version, factory);
  }

  create(type: string, version: number, payload: any): Command | null {
    return this.map.get(type)?.get(version)?.create(payload) || null;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design extensible CLI architectures with runtime plugin support and graceful user experience.

### Key Concepts
- **Command pattern**: Encapsulates operations as first-class objects
- **Registry**: Enables dynamic command registration and lookup
- **Alias support**: Handles naming conflicts and provides convenient shortcuts
- **Auto-generated help**: Constructed from command metadata
- **Graceful failure**: Provides helpful error messages for unrecognized commands

### Code Example
```typescript
interface CommandContext {
  args: Record<string, unknown>;
  stdin: NodeJS.ReadableStream;
  stdout: NodeJS.WritableStream;
  stderr: NodeJS.WritableStream;
}

interface CommandDefinition {
  name: string;
  aliases: string[];
  description: string;
  parse(args: string[]): Record<string, unknown>;
  execute(ctx: CommandContext): Promise<void> | void;
}

class CommandRegistry {
  private commands = new Map<string, CommandDefinition>();

  register(cmd: CommandDefinition): void {
    this.commands.set(cmd.name, cmd);
    cmd.aliases.forEach(alias => this.commands.set(alias, cmd));
  }

  resolve(name: string): CommandDefinition | undefined {
    return this.commands.get(name);
  }

  getCommands(): CommandDefinition[] {
    return [...new Set(this.commands.values())];
  }

  generateHelp(): string {
    const commands = this.getCommands();
    const longestName = Math.max(...commands.map(c => c.name.length));
    return [
      'Usage: cli <command> [options]',
      '',
      'Commands:',
      ...commands.map(c => `  ${c.name.padEnd(longestName)}  ${c.description}`),
    ].join('\n');
  }
}

async function main(argv: string[]) {
  const [cmdName, ...args] = argv.slice(2);
  const registry = new CommandRegistry();

  // Register commands
  registry.register({
    name: 'greet',
    aliases: ['g'],
    description: 'Greet a user',
    parse(args) {
      const name = args[0] || 'World';
      return { name };
    },
    execute({ args, stdout }) {
      stdout.write(`Hello, ${args.name}!\n`);
    },
  });

  if (!cmdName) {
    console.log(registry.generateHelp());
    process.exit(0);
  }

  const cmd = registry.resolve(cmdName);
  if (!cmd) {
    console.error(`Error: Unknown command '${cmdName}'`);
    console.error(registry.generateHelp());
    process.exit(1);
  }

  const parsedArgs = cmd.parse(args);
  cmd.execute({
    args: parsedArgs,
    stdin: process.stdin,
    stdout: process.stdout,
    stderr: process.stderr,
  });
}

main(process.argv);
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests designing an extensible authorization policy system that can evolve without touching core logic, mirroring real-world gateways.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy for concrete policies (RBAC, Time, Attribute)
- Composite for AND/OR
- Registry/Factory for runtime loading
- Thread-safety and immutability in evaluators

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Policy {
  evaluate(ctx: any): boolean;
}

class Registry {
  private factories = new Map<string, () => Policy>();
  register(name: string, factory: () => Policy) { this.factories.set(name, factory); }
  get(name: string): Policy { return this.factories.get(name)!(); }
}

class AndPolicy implements Policy {
  constructor(private a: Policy, private b: Policy) {}
  evaluate(ctx: any){ return this.a.evaluate(ctx) && this.b.evaluate(ctx); }
}

// Usage
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('rbac', () => new RolePolicy(['admin']));
registry.register('time', () => new TimeWindowPolicy('09:00-17:00'));
const policy = new AndPolicy(registry.get('rbac'), registry.get('time'));
policy.evaluate({ user: 'admin', time: '10:00' });
```

